pd.concat() in Pandas

pd.concat() is used to combine two or more DataFrames together.

The easiest way to remember:

concat() = concatenate / stack DataFrames

=====

1. Combine rows — axis=0

Suppose you have January and February sales:

import pandas as pd

jan = pd.DataFrame({
    "name": ["Sugumar", "Ravi"],
    "amount": [500, 700]
})

feb = pd.DataFrame({
    "name": ["Kumar", "Anu"],
    "amount": [600, 800]
})

=====

Use:

result = pd.concat([jan, feb])

Result:

|    | name    | amount |
| -: | ------- | -----: |
|  0 | Sugumar |    500 |
|  1 | Ravi    |    700 |
|  0 | Kumar   |    600 |
|  1 | Anu     |    800 |


It stacks one DataFrame below another.

===

Usually, reset the index:

result = pd.concat([jan, feb], ignore_index=True)

Result:

|    | name    | amount |
| -: | ------- | -----: |
|  0 | Sugumar |    500 |
|  1 | Ravi    |    700 |
|  2 | Kumar   |    600 |
|  3 | Anu     |    800 |


=====

. Combine columns — axis=1

Suppose:

customers = pd.DataFrame({
    "name": ["Sugumar", "Ravi"],
})

services = pd.DataFrame({
    "service": ["Haircut", "Facial"],
})

====

Use:

result = pd.concat([customers, services], axis=1)

Result:

|    | name    | service |
| -: | ------- | ------- |
|  0 | Sugumar | Haircut |
|  1 | Ravi    | Facial  |


Here the DataFrames are placed side by side.

=====


Real-time beauty parlour example 💇‍♀️

Imagine you maintain separate appointment DataFrames for two days.

Monday
monday = pd.DataFrame({
    "customer": ["Anu", "Priya"],
    "service": ["Facial", "Haircut"]
})
Tuesday
tuesday = pd.DataFrame({
    "customer": ["Divya", "Kavya"],
    "service": ["Manicure", "Pedicure"]
})

Combine them:

all_appointments = pd.concat(
    [monday, tuesday],
    ignore_index=True
)

Result:

|    | customer | service  |
| -: | -------- | -------- |
|  0 | Anu      | Facial   |
|  1 | Priya    | Haircut  |
|  2 | Divya    | Manicure |
|  3 | Kavya    | Pedicure |


====

merge() vs concat()

This is important:

pd.merge()
    ↓
Combine based on a COMMON KEY
    ↓
customer_id
pd.concat()
    ↓
Stack/combine DataFrames
    ↓
Rows or Columns

====

🔗 merge() → join using a key
📚 concat() → stack DataFrames

====

| Axis     | Direction | Think       |
| -------- | --------- | ----------- |
| `axis=0` | ⬇️ Down   | **Rows**    |
| `axis=1` | ➡️ Across | **Columns** |


====

For your pd.concat():

# Add rows
pd.concat([df1, df2], axis=0)

# Add columns
pd.concat([df1, df2], axis=1)

